# DataFlowBench benchmark-controlled modeling declarations for Pysa — Python.
#
# One block per scored template, delimited by `# template:` markers. The
# runner materializes exactly one block into each case workspace, because the
# pinned pair refuses a model that names a definition the sources do not
# carry (`Found N model verification errors!`, exit 10) — a whole-matrix
# model file would fail verification on every case, and a silently skipped
# model is precisely what that loud failure exists to prevent. The committed
# file is the modeling artifact: it is hash-bound whole into every report's
# `configuration_hash`, and the per-case selection is a mechanical cut at the
# markers, never an edit.
#
# The load-bearing-model requirement
# (docs/modeling-matrix.md#the-load-bearing-model-requirement) is carried by
# `@SkipAnalysis` + `@SkipObscure` on every declared propagator and summary
# entity. Amendment A16 measured why the engine needs the switch: the pinned
# pair resolves the matrix's reflective opaque body on its own (Pyrefly
# narrows `getattr(_impl, name)` over the local string constant), so without
# the skip modes the engine's own body reading — not the declaration — would
# decide every category P and O cell. `@SkipAnalysis` makes the declared
# entity's body ignored; `@SkipObscure` removes the engine's
# obscure-callable taint-through fallback that would otherwise replace it.
# Under both, the taint the entity carries is exactly the taint its
# declaration states — removing a `TaintInTaintOut` while keeping the skip
# modes was measured to flip the verdict, in both categories.
#
# Category B has no block: the partition declines it for this adapter
# (docs/modeling-matrix.md, Amendment A16). The `.pysa` DSL binds taint roles
# to callables and parameter positions and has no store identity, key, or
# cross-procedure link vocabulary to encode a persistence boundary in.

